CAT = "cscsHealth"; SEG = "HLT"
Q = [
("Buildings put up or refurbished before which year may contain asbestos?",
 "2000", ["1970", "1985", "2010"],
 "Asbestos was fully banned in the UK in 1999, so any building built or refurbished before 2000 may contain it. Check the asbestos survey before disturbing the fabric."),
("You uncover material you think may contain asbestos. What should you do?",
 "Stop work and tell your supervisor", ["Wet it and carry on carefully", "Bag it up and put it in the skip", "Sweep it up with a dry brush first"],
 "Stop work at once, keep others away and report it. Asbestos must be identified and dealt with by trained people under the Control of Asbestos Regulations 2012."),
("Which disease is caused by breathing in asbestos fibres?",
 "Mesothelioma", ["Dermatitis", "Leptospirosis", "Tinnitus"],
 "Asbestos fibres cause mesothelioma, asbestosis and lung cancer. Dermatitis is a skin condition and leptospirosis is spread by rat urine."),
("Asbestos-related diseases usually appear how long after exposure?",
 "15 to 60 years", ["1 to 6 months", "1 to 5 years", "A few weeks"],
 "Asbestos diseases usually appear 15 to 60 years after exposure, by which time it is too late to treat them. That is why exposure must be prevented."),
("Which regulations set out how asbestos must be managed and worked on?",
 "Control of Asbestos Regulations 2012", ["Control of Noise at Work Regulations 2005", "Work at Height Regulations 2005", "Manual Handling Regulations 1992"],
 "The Control of Asbestos Regulations 2012 cover duties to manage asbestos, licensing of higher-risk work, training and air monitoring."),
("Cutting concrete, brick or stone releases which harmful dust?",
 "Silica dust", ["Asbestos dust", "Wood dust", "Lead dust"],
 "Concrete, brick, stone and many blocks contain silica. The fine respirable dust released by cutting and grinding causes silicosis, COPD and lung cancer."),
("Which of these is the best way to control dust when cutting kerbs with a disc cutter?",
 "Water suppression on the saw", ["Cutting faster to cut less often", "Working with your back to the wind", "Wearing a hi-vis vest"],
 "Water suppression or on-tool extraction stops much of the dust at source. RPE should be worn as well, as engineering controls do not remove it all."),
("Why must a tight-fitting dust mask be face-fit tested?",
 "To check it seals to your face", ["To check it is the right colour", "To make it last longer", "To prove you have paid for it"],
 "A tight-fitting mask only protects you if it seals properly. A face-fit test checks the seal for your face; stubble or a beard can break it."),
("Why should you not use a dry brush to clean up fine dust from cutting?",
 "It puts the dust back into the air", ["It wears the brush out too fast", "It is slower than using a shovel", "It leaves streaks of wet dust behind"],
 "Dry sweeping lifts fine dust into the air where it can be breathed in. Use a suitable vacuum cleaner or damp methods."),
("Which disease is caused by breathing in silica dust?",
 "Silicosis", ["Asbestosis", "Weil's disease", "White finger"],
 "Silicosis is scarring of the lungs from silica dust. It is incurable and can be fatal. Asbestosis is caused by asbestos fibres."),
("At what daily noise exposure must your employer provide hearing protection and make sure it is worn?",
 "85 dB(A)", ["70 dB(A)", "75 dB(A)", "95 dB(A)"],
 "Under the Control of Noise at Work Regulations 2005 the upper exposure action value is 85 dB(A). At 80 dB(A) hearing protection must be made available on request."),
("What is a likely sign that noise on site is damaging your hearing?",
 "Ringing in your ears after work", ["Sore eyes at the end of the day", "Tingling in your fingers", "A dry cough in the morning"],
 "Ringing in the ears (tinnitus) or muffled hearing after work are warning signs. Noise-induced hearing loss is permanent."),
("What should you do with earplugs before fitting them?",
 "Make sure your hands are clean", ["Soak them in water for a minute", "Cut them to half length", "Wear them over your ear defenders"],
 "Dirty hands put dirt and germs into the ear canal. Roll the plug with clean hands and fit it fully as shown in your training."),
("You enter an area marked as a hearing protection zone. What must you do?",
 "Wear hearing protection", ["Turn your radio down", "Use hand signals only", "Leave within 10 minutes"],
 "Hearing protection zones are marked with a blue mandatory sign. Anyone entering must wear hearing protection, even for a short time."),
("Why must hearing protection be worn the whole time you are in a noisy area?",
 "Even short gaps give a big dose", ["It only works after warming up", "Your employer could be fined for it", "Noise levels change each hour"],
 "Removing protection for even a few minutes in a very noisy area greatly reduces the protection over the day. Keep it on until you leave the area."),
("Using vibrating tools such as breakers for long periods can cause which condition?",
 "Hand-arm vibration syndrome", ["Occupational asthma", "Chronic obstructive lung disease", "Allergic contact dermatitis"],
 "Hand-arm vibration syndrome (HAVS) damages blood vessels, nerves and joints in the hands and arms. It includes vibration white finger."),
("What is an early symptom of hand-arm vibration syndrome?",
 "Tingling and numb fingers", ["A ringing sound in the ears", "Red itchy skin on the arms", "Pain in the lower back"],
 "Tingling, numbness and fingers turning white in the cold are early signs of HAVS. Report them so your exposure can be reduced."),
("Which of these helps reduce the risk of hand-arm vibration syndrome?",
 "Low-vibration tools and regular breaks", ["Gripping the tool as tightly as you can", "Using a blunt bit to cut more slowly", "Working on with cold, wet hands"],
 "Low-vibration, well-maintained tools, job rotation and keeping warm all reduce HAVS risk. A tight grip and blunt bits increase vibration reaching your hands."),
("Why should vibrating tools be kept well maintained?",
 "Worn parts can increase vibration", ["They will be harder to steal", "It makes them louder and faster", "It means they no longer need a guard"],
 "Blunt bits, worn bearings and unbalanced parts increase vibration. Report faulty tools and use sharp, correct accessories."),
("Before using a hazardous substance such as a solvent, what should you check?",
 "The COSHH assessment for it", ["The price on the delivery note", "That nobody else is using it", "The colour of the container"],
 "The COSHH assessment tells you the hazards, the controls, the PPE to wear and what to do in an emergency. Read it before you start."),
("What does COSHH stand for?",
 "Control of Substances Hazardous to Health", ["Control of Site Health and Hygiene", "Code of Safe Handling of Hazards", "Control of Site Health Hazards"],
 "The Control of Substances Hazardous to Health Regulations 2002 require employers to assess and control the risks from hazardous substances."),
("Where would you find detailed information about the hazards of a chemical product?",
 "On its safety data sheet", ["On the delivery invoice", "In the daily site diary", "On the waste transfer note"],
 "The supplier's safety data sheet gives hazards, handling, storage, first aid and spill advice. It is used to prepare the COSHH assessment."),
("A container shows a red diamond with a skull and crossbones. What does it mean?",
 "The substance is acutely toxic", ["The substance is flammable", "The substance is an irritant", "The substance is under pressure"],
 "The skull and crossbones pictogram means acute toxicity: it can kill or harm in small amounts if swallowed, breathed in or absorbed."),
("Why should you never decant chemicals into drinks bottles?",
 "Someone may drink it by mistake", ["The bottle may leak over time", "It makes the chemical weaker", "It uses up too many spare bottles"],
 "Unlabelled chemicals in drinks bottles have caused poisonings. Keep substances in their original labelled containers."),
("Which health problem can be caused by regular skin contact with wet cement?",
 "Dermatitis and burns", ["Noise-induced hearing loss", "Vibration white finger", "Silicosis"],
 "Wet cement is strongly alkaline and can cause burns and dermatitis. Wear gloves and long sleeves and wash off any splashes quickly."),
("How can Weil's disease (leptospirosis) be caught on site?",
 "Water with rat urine in it", ["Breathing in concrete dust", "Touching live electrical cables", "Lifting heavy loads"],
 "Leptospirosis is spread by rat urine in water or wet ground and enters through cuts or the mouth. Cover cuts and wash your hands before eating."),
("What early symptoms of Weil's disease should you tell a doctor about?",
 "Flu-like symptoms", ["Ringing in the ears", "White fingers in the cold", "Dry, cracked skin"],
 "Leptospirosis starts like flu: fever, headache and aching muscles. Tell your doctor you work where rats may be present so it can be treated early."),
("Under the Manual Handling Regulations, what should your employer try to do first?",
 "Avoid the manual handling", ["Assess every lift in detail", "Give you a back support belt", "Limit loads to 50 kg"],
 "The order is: avoid hazardous manual handling where reasonably practicable, assess what cannot be avoided, then reduce the risk. Mechanical aids often remove the need to lift."),
("When lifting a load from the ground, how should you position yourself?",
 "Bend your knees, keep your back straight", ["Keep your legs straight and bend your back", "Twist at the waist as you lift it up", "Hold the load out at arm's length"],
 "Use your leg muscles, keep the load close to your body and avoid twisting. Bending the back puts the strain on your spine."),
("Why should you keep a load close to your body when carrying it?",
 "It puts less strain on your back", ["It stops the load getting dirty", "It lets you walk much faster", "It hides the load from other people"],
 "The further a load is from your body, the greater the force on your lower back. Close to the waist is the safest carrying position."),
("Before lifting a heavy object, what is the first thing you should do?",
 "Plan the lift and check the route", ["Lift it quickly to test the weight", "Ask someone to film the lift", "Remove your safety gloves"],
 "Think about the weight, your grip, where it is going and any obstructions. Get help or a lifting aid if needed."),
("What do the letters TILE stand for when assessing manual handling?",
 "Task, Individual, Load, Environment", ["Time, Injury, Lift, Effort", "Tools, Inspection, Load, Exit", "Training, Instruction, Lift, Equipment"],
 "TILE is a reminder to consider the task, the individual's capability, the load and the working environment."),
("A load is too heavy for you to lift alone. What should you do?",
 "Get help or use a lifting aid", ["Drag it across the floor", "Lift it in short jerks", "Leave it where it blocks the route"],
 "Use a trolley, hoist or team lift, planned so everyone lifts together. Jerking or dragging a heavy load risks injury."),
("Who must pay for the PPE your employer requires you to wear for work?",
 "Your employer", ["You, from your wages", "The client", "Your trade union"],
 "Employers must provide required PPE free of charge. The duty was extended in 2022 to limb (b) workers as well as employees."),
("Your safety helmet has a deep crack in the shell. What should you do?",
 "Get it replaced", ["Tape over the crack", "Keep using it until it breaks", "Swap it with a workmate's"],
 "A damaged helmet may not protect you. Report it and get a replacement; never paint, drill or modify a helmet."),
("Where in the order of controls does PPE come?",
 "Last, after other controls", ["First, before anything else", "Only if HSE insists on it", "Instead of a risk assessment"],
 "PPE only protects the wearer and only if worn correctly. It is the last resort after removing the hazard and using other controls."),
("Why must eye protection match the task?",
 "Different tasks need different protection", ["All glasses give the same protection", "Only colour matters for eye protection", "It must be the same brand as your helmet"],
 "Grinding, chemicals and welding need different grades of eye protection. Check the risk assessment for the right type."),
("Which kind of gloves should you wear when using solvents?",
 "Chemical-resistant gloves", ["Cotton knitted work gloves", "Leather rigger gloves", "Fingerless thermal gloves"],
 "Solvents pass through cotton and leather. Use the chemical-resistant type named in the COSHH assessment."),
("What should you do if your PPE does not fit properly?",
 "Get PPE that fits", ["Wear it anyway", "Adjust it with tape", "Stop wearing it"],
 "PPE that does not fit will not protect you and may create new hazards. Your employer must provide PPE that fits."),
("You need to wear a dust mask and safety glasses together. What should you check?",
 "That they work together without gaps", ["That they are both the same colour", "That both were bought new this week", "That the mask covers both of your eyes"],
 "Different items of PPE must be compatible. Glasses can stop a mask sealing, and a mask can push glasses out of place."),
]
